ui2/persistence/src/main/java/.../artefact/BackupArtefactRetrieval.java, the tail of retrieve(), verbatim:

        Path destination = Path.of(destinationPath);
        try (InputStream decrypted = artefactStore.retrieve(new ArtefactRef(artefactId), manifest.get().wrappedDataKey(),
                false);
                OutputStream out = Files.newOutputStream(destination)) {
            decrypted.transferTo(out);
        } catch (IOException e) {
            return new RetrieveResult.IoFailure(String.valueOf(e.getMessage()));
        }

        retrievalRepository.record(UUID.randomUUID().toString(), artefactId, reason, destinationPath,
                actorFingerprint, "backup_artefact_retrieved");
        return new RetrieveResult.Ok(destinationPath);

The decrypted bytes reach the destination before record() is called. If record() throws, the file is on disk and nothing says who took it or why.

The checks that come first are already correct and stay: a reason shorter than the minimum is refused, an actor without an active backup_admin binding is refused, and a missing manifest is refused. Only the ordering of the write and the audit is wrong.

The result vocabulary is a sealed set: ReasonTooShort, RoleRefused, ArtefactNotFound, IoFailure, Ok.
